@page "/getting-started"
@inject IBonboardingService BonboardingService

<PageTitle>Getting started · Bonboarding</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Getting started</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        Four pieces of wiring and a first tour: install the package, register the services, reference the
        stylesheet and host one <code>BonboardingRoot</code> - then mark an element and point a step at it.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Install the package</h3>
    <p>Bit.Bonboarding targets .NET 10 and .NET 11, and depends on nothing but Blazor itself.</p>
    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0">dotnet add package Bit.Bonboarding</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Register the services</h3>
    <p>
        Everything is registered as scoped - per circuit on Blazor Server, per application on WebAssembly - which
        matches the lifetime of a user's onboarding state. Global defaults are set in the same call.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0"><span class="k">using</span> Bit.Bonboarding;

builder.Services.AddBitBonboardingServices(options =>
{
    options.Placement = BonboardingPlacement.Bottom;
    options.Progress = BonboardingProgressStyle.Bar;
    options.Labels.Skip = <span class="s">"Not now"</span>;
    options.StorageKeyPrefix = <span class="s">"acme:onboarding:"</span>;
});</pre>
    <div class="bb-note bb-note-warn">
        A Blazor Web App has two projects and two DI containers: register in <strong>both</strong>, with the same
        options. While a page prerenders, every component runs on the server, on the server project's registration and
        options; once the page turns interactive, an <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code> component switches to the client
        project's. So a registration missing in the server project fails the page while it prerenders, and one missing in
        the client project only once the page turns interactive on WebAssembly - a Bonboarding component with an error
        that names <code>AddBitBonboardingServices</code>, a component that injects <code>IBonboardingService</code> with
        Blazor's own "no registered service" error. This site
        does it through one extension method both <code>Program.cs</code> files call. Only a WebAssembly app that
        <a href="getting-started#lazy-loading">lazy loads</a> the library leaves it out.
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Reference the stylesheet</h3>
    <p>
        In <code>App.razor</code> (Blazor Web App) or <code>index.html</code> (WebAssembly), before your own stylesheet
        so your overrides of its <code>--bit-bonboarding-*</code> variables win. There is no script tag to add: the
        library imports its own JavaScript module.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0">&lt;<span class="k">link</span> rel=<span class="s">"stylesheet"</span> href=<span class="s">"@@Assets["_content/Bit.Bonboarding/bit-bonboarding.css"]"</span> /&gt;</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Host one BonboardingRoot</h3>
    <p>
        One host renders whichever tour is running, from anywhere in the application. Put it somewhere that outlives
        navigation - the main layout, or beside the router as this site does - because a host on a page would be
        disposed by the navigation a multi-page tour performs. Then import the namespace in <code>_Imports.razor</code>.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0"><span class="c">@@* MainLayout.razor *@@</span>
&lt;<span class="k">div</span> class=<span class="s">"page"</span>&gt;
    @@Body
&lt;/<span class="k">div</span>&gt;

&lt;<span class="k">BonboardingRoot</span> /&gt;

<span class="c">@@* _Imports.razor *@@</span>
@@using Bit.Bonboarding</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Write a first tour</h3>
    <p>
        Mark the element a step is about with <code>data-bonboarding-anchor</code>, then start a tour that points at it.
        This is the setup check from the guide, running on this page: if the card appears next to the button, with
        the spotlight around it and the arrow keys working, the wiring is complete.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span></div>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" data-bonboarding-anchor="probe" @onclick="RunAsync">Test the setup</button>
    </div>

    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0">@ProbeExample</pre>

    <div class="bb-note">
        If the card appears but sits in the wrong place, the stylesheet is not loaded. If nothing appears at all,
        <code>BonboardingRoot</code> is missing from the layout - or the component that starts the tour is not interactive.
    </div>

    <p>
        The same tour can be declared in markup with <code>&lt;BonboardingTour&gt;</code> and
        <code>&lt;BonboardingStep&gt;</code> - the landing page's welcome tour is written that way.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Pointing at an element</h3>
    <p>
        A step's <code>Target</code> says which element it is about. Each button below is found a different way; the
        tour visits all three and ends on a step with no target at all, which is drawn as a centred card - the right
        shape for a welcome or a summary.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <div class="bb-demo-bar">
            <span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="RunTargetingAsync">Run the targeting tour</button>
        </div>
        <div class="bb-toolbar">
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @attributes="BitBonboarding.Anchor(TargetingAnchor)">By anchor</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" id="targeting-by-selector">By CSS selector</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @ref="_byElement">By element reference</button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Target kinds">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Target</th><th>Finds</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>Anchor("name")</code></td>
                    <td>
                        The element carrying <code>data-bonboarding-anchor="name"</code>. <strong>Preferred</strong>: it is
                        looked up again on every measurement, so it survives re-renders and elements that appear later.
                        <code>BitBonboarding.Anchor("name")</code> splats the attribute for you.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>Css("#id .cls")</code></td>
                    <td>The first rendered element matching a CSS selector. A plain string converts to one implicitly.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>Element(reference)</code></td>
                    <td>
                        A captured <code>ElementReference</code>. It names one DOM node, so it goes stale if Blazor replaces
                        that node - use it for elements that are rendered once and stay.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>Dynamic(resolver)</code></td>
                    <td>Decided when the step activates. See <a href="dynamic">dynamic targets</a>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key"><code>None</code></td>
                    <td>No element: a centred card with no spotlight and no arrow. The default.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0">@TargetingExample</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Where to start a tour</h3>
    <p>
        The library is prerender-safe, but <code>OnInitializedAsync</code> runs during prerendering and again after
        hydration - in two different scopes. Start tours from <code>OnAfterRenderAsync(firstRender: true)</code>, from a
        user action, or with <code>AutoStart</code>, which already does the right thing.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0">&lt;<span class="k">BonboardingTour</span> Id=<span class="s">"welcome"</span> AutoStart=<span class="s">"BonboardingAutoStart.Once"</span>&gt;
    ...
&lt;/<span class="k">BonboardingTour</span>&gt;

<span class="k">await</span> BonboardingService.StartAsync(tour);       <span class="c">// always start, resuming saved progress</span>
<span class="k">await</span> BonboardingService.StartOnceAsync(tour);   <span class="c">// only if not already completed, dismissed or snoozed</span>
<span class="k">await</span> BonboardingService.RestartAsync(tour);     <span class="c">// forget progress and start from the top</span></pre>
    <p>
        <code>AutoStart</code> is <code>None</code> (the default), <code>Once</code> - per user, remembered by the store -
        or <code>Always</code>, every time the page renders.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-note">
        On Blazor Server, a dropped circuit is rebuilt in a new scope; because progress is stored after every step, a
        reconnected user resumes where they were. Under a Content Security Policy, the library needs its own ES module
        from your origin and inline <code>style</code> attributes for positioning - a policy that allows Blazor allows
        both, unless its <code>style-src</code> forbids <code>unsafe-inline</code>.
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Let people replay it</h3>
    <p>
        A tour is known by its id, so a help menu anywhere in the application can start it again. A tour declared in
        markup is registered only while its page is on screen; register the ones a help menu should always offer at
        startup. A ready-made list of tours with Start, Resume and Replay buttons is on the
        <a href="guidance">guidance page</a>.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0">@ReplayExample</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card" id="lazy-loading">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Lazy loading on WebAssembly</h3>
    <p>
        A WebAssembly app can fetch Bonboarding only when someone opens a part of the app that has tours. Such an app cannot
        call <code>AddBitBonboardingServices</code> - <code>Program.cs</code> runs before the assembly is there - so it
        turns on the <code>Bit.Bonboarding.LazyLoad</code> runtime switch instead. With the switch on and the services not
        registered, <code>BonboardingRoot</code>, <code>BonboardingTour</code>, <code>BonboardingBeacon</code>,
        <code>BonboardingChecklist</code> and <code>BonboardingTourList</code> create every service they need themselves,
        once per browser connection, and <code>IServiceProvider.GetBonboardingService()</code> hands the app's own code the
        same <code>IBonboardingService</code>. A tour's hooks still reach the app's own services through their context.
        In the project that runs in the browser, mark the assembly as lazy loaded and turn the switch on, then load the
        assembly before the first page that uses it renders:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0">@LazyProjectExample</pre>
    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0">@LazyRouterExample</pre>
    <p>
        Those pages, or a layout only they use, host the tours and reach the service through
        <code>GetBonboardingService()</code> instead of injecting <code>IBonboardingService</code>:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0">@LazyLayoutExample</pre>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Leave <code>AddBitBonboardingServices</code> out of that project's <code>Program.cs</code>: calling it references Bonboarding's types before the assembly is there. For the same reason only what renders after the assembly has loaded may use Bonboarding's types - the pages it is loaded for and a layout only they use, not <code>App.razor</code>, a layout every page shares or <code>Program.cs</code>. That includes <code>&lt;BonboardingRoot /&gt;</code>: it goes in that layout, not in the main one.</li>
        <li>The switch is what tells a lazy loading app apart from one that forgot the registration: without it, a missing registration fails with an error that names <code>AddBitBonboardingServices</code>, as it does everywhere else. <code>AppContext.SetSwitch("Bit.Bonboarding.LazyLoad", true);</code> at the top of <code>Program.cs</code> turns it on too, without referencing Bonboarding's types.</li>
        <li>No type in an assembly the router reads when the app starts - the app's own assembly first of all - may derive from or implement one of Bonboarding's: no <code>IBonboardingObserver</code>, <code>IBonboardingStore</code> or <code>IBonboardingLocalizer</code> class. The router reads the types of those assemblies at startup, and on WebAssembly a type whose base type or interface lives in an assembly that is not loaded yet fails to load - and keeps failing after Bit.Bonboarding has loaded, breaking the app's use of it for the rest of the session. Put such a class in a library that is lazy loaded together with Bit.Bonboarding.</li>
        <li>The stylesheet is a plain <code>&lt;link&gt;</code>, served whether the assembly has loaded or not, and the components import their JavaScript module the first time they need it.</li>
        <li>Without the registration the components run on the default <code>BonboardingOptions</code>. A tour and a step override most of them - every <code>BonboardingStepOptions</code> member, <code>Labels</code> and <code>Persist</code> - on their definition or as parameters of <code>BonboardingTour</code> and <code>BonboardingStep</code>. The rest - <code>StorageKeyPrefix</code>, <code>StorageLocation</code>, <code>StorageScope</code>, <code>AutoNavigateToStepRoute</code> and <code>EnableJsDiagnostics</code> - keep their defaults; an app that needs them registers the services rather than lazy loading.</li>
        <li>The store, the localizer and the observers cannot be replaced either: progress goes to the browser's <code>localStorage</code> under the default <code>bit-bonboarding:</code> prefix, which every app on the same origin shares, for the device as a whole rather than per user. A tour that must not write to the browser sets <code>Persist = false</code>; analytics subscribe to <code>GetBonboardingService().EventRaised</code> instead of registering an observer.</li>
        <li>Do not lazy load in an AOT build. AOT compiles every assembly into <code>dotnet.native.wasm</code>, so there is nothing left to download later, and lazy loading an AOT-compiled assembly can fail at startup.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

